title: On Hipsters
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

I was having a discussion with a friend over beers. He was explaining to me his ideas about what being a hipster means… It was interesting stuff. Thinking about how we consume, buy, etc. and what that is supposed to mean about us. People spend their money on cars, houses, name brand clothes, etc. all as objects of proof of some social station or class level or similar nonsense. In this same way these "hipsters" — this must have been 2005 or 2006, right as the neo-Brooklyn hipster thing was taking off — chased rare records, vintage jackets, obscure films, whatever half-empty show was happening downtown that night, in a city where all of that was actually findable. Money bought some of it, but mostly it took time — digging through bins, showing up to the right small room before anyone else knew about it. The status object wasn't the car anymore. It was how much time you'd spent finding something nobody else had.
